---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-e`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin E Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Vitamin E', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin E: Alpha-Tocopherol, Antioxidant Evidence, and Safe Doses"
  description="Vitamin E reference — RDA, dietary sources, RRR- vs synthetic tocopherol, mixed tocopherols, and why high-dose supplements failed in cardiovascular and cancer trials."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/vitamin-e", label: "Vitamin E" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin E</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Vitamin E is a family of eight related compounds — four tocopherols and four
      tocotrienols. Alpha-tocopherol is the form most biologically active in humans and the
      one measured in blood tests. Its main role is as a lipid-phase antioxidant, protecting
      cell membranes from oxidative damage. Deficiency is rare in the general population;
      supplementation trials have been unexpectedly disappointing.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily requirements</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Adults:</strong> 15 mg alpha-tocopherol/day (~22 IU natural, ~33 IU synthetic).</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 15 mg.</li>
      <li><strong>Lactation:</strong> 19 mg.</li>
      <li><strong>Tolerable Upper Intake Level:</strong> 1,000 mg/day supplemental — high-dose is associated with bleeding risk.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Natural vs synthetic</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Natural vitamin E is <strong>d-alpha-tocopherol</strong> (or RRR-alpha-tocopherol) —
      one stereoisomer. Synthetic vitamin E is <strong>dl-alpha-tocopherol</strong> — a
      mixture of eight stereoisomers, of which only the RRR isomer is fully bioactive. On a
      per-mg basis, natural has about 1.5× the biological activity of synthetic. Both are
      safe; food-derived forms are best.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The failed supplementation trials</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      Vitamin E was one of the most heavily studied antioxidant supplements of the 2000s.
      Results were consistently null or slightly harmful for the general population:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>HOPE-TOO trial</strong> (n=9,541, 7 years, 400 IU/day) — no reduction in cardiovascular events; increased heart failure.</li>
      <li><strong>SELECT trial</strong> (n=35,533, 5.5 years, 400 IU/day) — no cancer prevention; 17% increase in prostate cancer risk on longer follow-up.</li>
      <li><strong>Miller meta-analysis</strong> (2005) — high-dose vitamin E (&gt; 400 IU/day) associated with 4% increase in all-cause mortality.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">
      The current consensus: <strong>do not take high-dose vitamin E supplements</strong>
      for prevention. Standard multivitamin doses (~30 IU) are safe.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Clinical uses of vitamin E</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH)</strong> — 800 IU/day slows progression in non-diabetic adults.</li>
      <li><strong>Abetalipoproteinaemia and rare fat-malabsorption disorders</strong> — high-dose vitamin E prevents neurological deterioration.</li>
      <li><strong>Age-related macular degeneration</strong> — vitamin E is a component of the AREDS-2 formula (400 IU) that slows progression.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Food sources</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Wheat germ oil (1 tbsp): 20 mg</li>
      <li>Sunflower seeds (1 oz): 7.4 mg</li>
      <li>Almonds (1 oz): 6.8 mg</li>
      <li>Sunflower oil (1 tbsp): 5.6 mg</li>
      <li>Hazelnuts (1 oz): 4.3 mg</li>
      <li>Peanut butter, spinach, broccoli, avocado — 1–3 mg per serving</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Vitamin E supplementation above 400 IU/day is not
      recommended without a specific clinical indication. It increases bleeding risk in
      patients on anticoagulants (warfarin, DOACs, aspirin).
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-e" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
